  "revision": "2026-10-24.2",
  "changeRecord": "Crawls through a phone's tunnel are checked against the address the phone reached. Since 2026-08-18.1 the initial navigation and the model's navigate action assert that the browser's peer address is public, but through the device-proxy tunnel that peer is always the engine's own SOCKS5 relay at 127.0.0.1, so every device-proxy crawl to a host not declared private was refused at its first page. TunnelService now reports, in the connected frame it already sends, the address its socket reached; the engine accepts only an IP address there, canonicalises it, and records it against the host and port the browser asked for; crawl-executor marks a proxied browser context as tunnelled; and for that context the check reads the phone's reports for the host the response came from, which is also the host a declaration must name, and refuses with ConnectedAddressRefusedError, so a refusal through the tunnel ends the crawl as revision 2026-10-24.1 made every such refusal do. A companion that reports nothing leaves the pre-flight name check standing alone, as every crawl had before 2026-08-18.1. No prompt, schema, identity, accumulation, extraction or OTP-relay behaviour changes, and a direct crawl is checked exactly as before. Verified through the engine's real tunnel server, SOCKS5 relay, crawl executor, agent loop, error recovery and Chrome, with a scripted model whose inputs are recorded: a reported internal address ends the crawl before the model is shown the page, on the first page, a navigate, a recovery retry and a redirect to an undeclared host; a public one is allowed; a redirect to a declared host is followed; and mutations that restore the previous check are each rejected by those tests. Pins moved: apps/engine/src/crawl-executor.ts, companion/android/app/src/main/kotlin/app/accrawl/accrawl_companion/TunnelService.kt. Previous revision: 2026-10-24.1.",
  "baselineRevision": "ee1f6bf05dae9c6deb856cd6703fd00f463338b5",
